{"causes": ["The escalating tensions between Athens and Sparta directly led to the outbreak of the First Peloponnesian War and Cleandridas's military deployment.", "The strategic importance of Megara as a buffer state and a key route between the Peloponnese and Attica necessitated Spartan intervention.", "The Spartan political system, which appointed ephors as supreme magistrates, granted Cleandridas significant military authority during his campaign."], "effects": ["Cleandridas's controversial withdrawal from Attica led to his condemnation for bribery and subsequent exile from Sparta.", "The temporary relief of Megara from sustained Athenian pressure allowed for a brief respite in the conflict over the city.", "His actions contributed to the eventual negotiation of the Thirty Years' Peace between Athens and Sparta in 446/445 BCE, albeit a fragile one."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "cleandridas", "sourceName": "Cleandridas", "verb": "PARTICIPATES_IN", "targetSlug": "first-peloponnesian-war", "targetName": "First Peloponnesian War", "context": "Cleandridas served as a Spartan general during the First Peloponnesian War, notably leading an army into Attica in 446 BCE."}, {"sourceSlug": "spartan-ephorate", "sourceName": "Spartan Ephorate", "verb": "DEFINES", "targetSlug": "cleandridas", "targetName": "Cleandridas", "context": "Cleandridas held the powerful position of ephor, which granted him significant executive and military authority within the Spartan political system."}, {"sourceSlug": "cleandridas", "sourceName": "Cleandridas", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "megara", "targetName": "Megara", "context": "Cleandridas's military campaign in 446 BCE was primarily focused on relieving the strategic city of Megara from Athenian pressure."}, {"sourceSlug": "pericles", "sourceName": "Pericles", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "cleandridas", "targetName": "Cleandridas", "context": "Pericles allegedly bribed Cleandridas to withdraw his Spartan forces from Attica in 446 BCE, thereby influencing his military decision."}, {"sourceSlug": "cleandridas", "sourceName": "Cleandridas", "verb": "CAUSES", "targetSlug": "exile-to-thurii", "targetName": "Exile to Thurii", "context": "Cleandridas's controversial actions and alleged corruption led directly to his condemnation and subsequent exile to the colony of Thurii."}], "places": [{"name": "Sparta, Greece", "role": "Home city, source of command"}, {"name": "Megara, Greece", "role": "Site of military action"}, {"name": "Thurii, Italy", "role": "Place of exile"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Cleandridas", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "cleandridas"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q475679", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cleandridas"], "tags": ["Military Leaders & Commanders", "conflict-and-resolution", "geopolitical-linkage", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 6, "significanceNarrative": "Cleandridas's actions directly influenced the immediate outcome of a critical phase of the First Peloponnesian War, temporarily averting a major confrontation and contributing to a temporary peace. His subsequent disgrace also highlighted the strict ethical expectations within the Spartan military and political system.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-26T09:13:57Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#45789659177", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Cleandridas (b. 500 BCE) was a public servant or civil administrator of the Classical era, an age of empires, philosophical inquiry, and foundational civilizations, active in Global. Known in the fields of military leaders & commanders.", "newValue": "Cleandridas, a Spartan ephor and military commander active around 446 BCE, played a pivotal, albeit controversial, role in the early stages of the First Peloponnesian War. Entrusted with leading a Spartan-Peloponnesian army into Attica, his actions significantly influenced the trajectory of the conflict between Athens and Sparta, particularly concerning the strategic city of Megara. His career exemplifies the intricate balance of military command, political oversight, and personal ambition within the rigid Spartan system, highlighting the pressures and temptations faced by leaders in an era of escalating interstate rivalry.

In 446 BCE, Cleandridas was dispatched from the Peloponnese with a substantial force, ostensibly to aid Megara, which had revolted from the Athenian hegemony and was under Athenian siege. His primary objective was to relieve Megara and potentially threaten Athens itself. However, instead of engaging the Athenian forces directly, Cleandridas, allegedly influenced by a substantial bribe from the Athenian general Pericles, withdrew his troops after only a brief show of force. This unexpected retreat allowed Athens to consolidate its control over Megara and its environs, albeit temporarily, and avoided a major confrontation that could have irrevocably escalated the war at that time.

